    "q-44172": {
      "dom_id": "step-q44172",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Factor ${x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
