    "q-78927": {
      "dom_id": "step-q78927",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Factor ${x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+\\frac{25}{36}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+\\frac{25}{36}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{5}{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
